+## Deploy
+
+Fizzy deploys to one Linux server with Docker using [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org). SQLite, the web process, and background jobs share a single container. The proxy terminates SSL.
+
+Fill in your hostname and mail server in [`config/deploy.yml`](config/deploy.yml), fill in [`.kamal/secrets`](.kamal/secrets), then run `bin/kamal setup`. Fizzy will be at `https://<your hostname>`, and the SQLite databases survive the next deploy. See the [deployment guide](docs/deployment.md).
+
 ## Contributing
 
 We welcome contributions! Please read our [style guide](STYLE.md) before submitting code.
diff --git a/config/deploy.yml b/config/deploy.yml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a442172
--- /dev/null
+++ b/config/deploy.yml
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+# One-box Kamal deploy.
+#
+# A single Linux server with Docker. SQLite, the web process, and background
+# jobs all run in one container. kamal-proxy terminates SSL. Uploads and the
+# SQLite databases live in the fizzy_storage volume, so they survive the next
+# deploy.
+#
+# Before the first boot, fill in:
+#   1. Your hostname (the anchor below). DNS must point at the server.
+#   2. Your mail server (SMTP_ADDRESS and MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS).
+#   3. Your secrets in .kamal/secrets.
+#
+# Then, from this repo:
+#
+#   bin/kamal setup
+#
+# Fizzy will be at https://<your hostname>. Later deploys are `bin/kamal deploy`.
+
+service: fizzy
+
+# Local registry (started by Kamal). No Docker Hub account required.
+# To push to an external registry instead, set image to your-user/fizzy and
+# replace this block with a username and a password secret.
+image: fizzy
+
+# SSH destination. Replace fizzy.example.com with the hostname that points at
+# this server. The &host anchor is also the public name used for SSL and URLs.
+# If you SSH to a different address, replace only this list entry (drop &host)
+# and set proxy.host and APP_HOST to the public hostname.
+servers:
+  web:
+    - &host fizzy.example.com
+
+# Let's Encrypt via kamal-proxy. Requires ports 80 and 443 open on the server.
+# If you use Cloudflare, set SSL/TLS encryption mode to "Full".
+proxy:
+  ssl: true
+  host: *host
+
+registry:
+  server: localhost:5555
+
+env:
+  clear:
+    RAILS_ENV: production
+
+    # Hostname for magic-link and notification URLs. Kept in lockstep with proxy.host.
+    APP_HOST: *host
+
+    # SQLite on the persistent volume. Do not point this at MySQL for a one-box deploy.
+    DATABASE_ADAPTER: sqlite
+    ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE: local
+
+    # Jobs run inside the web container (Solid Queue + Puma), on this same box.
+    # A separate jobs container cannot share this SQLite database safely.
+    SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: "true"
+    JOB_CONCURRENCY: 1
+
+    # Mail server. Magic-link sign-in needs this. Put credentials in .kamal/secrets.
+    MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS: Fizzy <fizzy@example.com>
+    SMTP_ADDRESS: smtp.example.com
+    SMTP_PORT: 587
+    SMTP_DOMAIN: *host
+  secret:
+    - SECRET_KEY_BASE
+    - SMTP_USERNAME
+    - SMTP_PASSWORD
+
+# Named Docker volume. Kamal replaces the container on each deploy; this volume stays.
+volumes:
+  - fizzy_storage:/rails/storage
+
+# Keep fingerprinted assets available across the cutover so in-flight requests do not 404.
+asset_path: /rails/public/assets
+
+builder:
+  # Match the server. Use arm64 for ARM hosts (Oracle, Hetzner ARM, Raspberry Pi).
+  arch: amd64
+
+aliases:
+  console: app exec --interactive --reuse "bin/rails console"
+  shell: app exec --interactive --reuse "bash"
+  logs: app logs -f
+  dbc: app exec --interactive --reuse "bin/rails dbconsole"
+
+# Use a non-root SSH user with Docker access:
+# ssh:
+#   user: app
diff --git a/config/environments/production.rb b/config/environments/production.rb
index 7e81725..fe81682 100644
--- a/config/environments/production.rb
+++ b/config/environments/production.rb
@@ -80,6 +80,46 @@ Rails.application.configure do
   # Set this to true and configure the email server for immediate delivery to raise delivery errors.
   # config.action_mailer.raise_delivery_errors = false
 
+  # Canonical host for generated URLs (magic links, notifications). Set by Kamal.
+  if (app_host = ENV["APP_HOST"].to_s.sub(%r{\Ahttps?://}i, "").split("/").first.presence)
+    url_options = { host: app_host, protocol: "https" }
+    config.action_mailer.default_url_options = url_options
+    config.action_controller.default_url_options = url_options
+    config.hosts << app_host
+    # kamal-proxy health checks hit the container directly and may not send this host.
+    config.host_authorization = { exclude: ->(request) { request.path == "/up" } }
+
+    config.after_initialize do
+      Rails.application.routes.default_url_options = url_options
+    end
+  end
+
+  # Mail server from the Kamal env (see config/deploy.yml). Unset during asset precompile.
+  if ENV["SMTP_ADDRESS"].present?
+    smtp_settings = {
+      address: ENV["SMTP_ADDRESS"],
+      port: Integer(ENV["SMTP_PORT"].presence || 587),
+      domain: ENV["SMTP_DOMAIN"].presence || ENV["APP_HOST"].presence,
+      user_name: ENV["SMTP_USERNAME"].presence,
+      password: ENV["SMTP_PASSWORD"].presence,
+      enable_starttls_auto: ENV.fetch("SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO", "true") == "true"
+    }
+
+    if ENV["SMTP_USERNAME"].present?
+      smtp_settings[:authentication] = ENV.fetch("SMTP_AUTHENTICATION", "plain").to_sym
+    end
+
+    if ENV["SMTP_TLS"] == "true"
+      smtp_settings[:tls] = true
+      smtp_settings[:enable_starttls_auto] = false
+    end
+
+    config.action_mailer.delivery_method = :smtp
+    config.action_mailer.perform_deliveries = true
+    config.action_mailer.raise_delivery_errors = true
+    config.action_mailer.smtp_settings = smtp_settings.compact
+  end
+
   # Enable locale fallbacks for I18n (makes lookups for any locale fall back to
   # the I18n.default_locale when a translation cannot be found).
   config.i18n.fallbacks = true
diff --git a/docs/deployment.md b/docs/deployment.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ed17398
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+## Deploying to one box
+
+This is the path for a fork that wants Fizzy on a single Linux server. You need Docker on that server, and Docker on the machine you deploy from (Kamal builds the image there). SQLite, uploads, the web process, and background jobs all run in one container. [kamal-proxy](https://github.com/basecamp/kamal-proxy) terminates SSL.
+
+37signals' own production setup lives in the `saas/` gem and is not what you want for this. Leave `SAAS` unset.
+
+### Fill in three things
+
+1. **Hostname.** In `config/deploy.yml`, replace `fizzy.example.com` with the hostname that points at the server. That value is the SSH target, the Let's Encrypt name, and the host used in generated URLs. Ports 80 and 443 must reach the server. If you SSH somewhere else (an IP, a bastion), put that address in `servers.web`, drop the `&host` anchor, and set `proxy.host` and `APP_HOST` to the public hostname.
+
+2. **Mail server.** Still in `config/deploy.yml`, set `SMTP_ADDRESS` to your mail server and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` to the address Fizzy should send from. Sign-in is a magic link, so mail has to work. Port 587 with STARTTLS is the default. For implicit TLS on port 465, also set `SMTP_TLS: "true"`.
+
+3. **Secrets.** In `.kamal/secrets`, set `SECRET_KEY_BASE` (generate one with `bin/rails secret` and keep it stable across deploys). If the mail server requires authentication, set `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` too. Pull values from the environment or a password manager. Do not commit raw secrets.
+
+### Boot
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+That is the same command as `kamal setup` if the binstub is on your `PATH`. Kamal installs the proxy, builds the image, starts the container, and runs database migrations. Fizzy is then at `https://<your hostname>`.
+
+Later deploys:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+### What persists
+
+SQLite databases and on-disk uploads live under `/rails/storage` in the container, mounted from the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume. Replacing the container on the next deploy does not delete that volume. Back it up out of band if you care about the data.
+
+Background jobs (Solid Queue, including the recurring tasks in `config/recurring.yml`) run inside the web container on this server. Do not add a separate jobs host: two containers cannot share this SQLite database safely.
+
+### Useful commands
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs
+bin/kamal console
+bin/kamal shell
+```
